window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][47] = "<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd start with the criteria the team set at the beginning: what thresholds on retention or the disappointment survey would have meant stop? If none were set, I'd write them now, before looking at more data, to avoid moving the goalposts. Then I'd read the data properly: cohort retention (flat overall may hide a segment that retains), the survey by segment, and who the retained users are.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then the decision table. Weak everywhere on tests that were well designed means kill; strong in a narrow segment means pivot to that segment; inconclusive because the test was poor means persist with a better test. Suppose retention flattens at 11% and one segment is 46% very disappointed against 18% elsewhere: that is a pivot, treated as a hypothesis with new kill criteria, say retention of 20% in that segment at week eight, and a time box of eight weeks. I'd also name sunk cost: the six months are gone either way and shouldn't weigh.</p>\n<p>&quot;For the team: I'd separate the idea from the people, thank them for what was learned, and make sure that stopping is seen as the result of a good test, not a failure.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Pre-set criteria; segment reading; the decision table; sunk cost; a time box; team care.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if there are no segments with strong signal? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then it is a kill, unless the test design was weak. I'd check whether the audience was right and the test long enough; if both are fine, stop, capture the learning and free the team for the next bet.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you stop a pivot from being endless wandering? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Each pivot is a hypothesis with a threshold and a deadline; the team keeps a log of what each test showed, and leadership agrees a limit on pivots before the funding or time runs out.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c26-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C26-07 · Hard · product-sense · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You are asked to build a product for smallholder farmers in a region where there is little data. What do you build first?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;An app that connects farmers to buyers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts with research on the users and the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Researches the farmers' actual jobs and constraints (connectivity, trust, payments, language), identifies the riskiest assumption, runs an assisted concierge test through channels they already use, measures real transactions, and plans how to scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd begin with the farmers, not the app: observe and interview 20 to 30 farmers about how they sell today, to whom, at what price, how they get paid, what goes wrong and what they would pay or give up to fix it. The constraints matter: patchy connectivity, low-end phones, limited literacy in some languages, trust in middlemen and in digital payments (C09).</p>\n<p>&quot;Assumptions: farmers want a different way to sell (value); they'll use a channel they already know (usability: voice or a messaging app, not a new app); buyers will pay enough for reliable supply (viability); logistics and payment can be handled (feasibility). The riskiest is probably trust and the transaction itself: will a farmer sell through us and will the buyer pay?</p>\n<p>&quot;First build: a concierge marketplace through a phone line and a messaging group. We take produce listings from 40 farmers, match them with 6 buyers by hand, and handle payment through a method both use. Measure: listings per farmer, the share of listings sold, price compared with the farmer's usual price, repeat sales in four weeks. Thresholds: proceed if 30% of farmers (12 of 40) sell at least twice and the average price is at least 10% better than the middleman price. The learning is what to automate: matching, price information, payment. I'd decide the app only after the manual process works.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Research first; local constraints; riskiest assumption; a concierge test with thresholds.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid assumptions based on your own context? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By spending time in the field, with local researchers and community members, listening for what they already do, and by testing in their channels. I would also involve them in judging the design, and I'd keep my assumptions written and test them.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the middlemen resist? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;They may be partners as well as competitors: they provide credit, transport and trust. I'd learn what they do for farmers before trying to replace them; a product that includes them may work better than one that excludes them.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c26-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C26-08 · Intermediate · product-sense · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You have two zero-to-one ideas and little data on either. How do you decide which to pursue?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pick the bigger market.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares on criteria and effort.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses evidence-adjusted scoring and a simple expected-value comparison including the cost and speed of testing, prefers the idea with faster learning when values are close, and considers reversibility and learning value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd compare them on the criteria with evidence quality, then on expected value and learning speed. Suppose idea X has a 30% chance of working and a payoff of $5 million, and idea Y a 15% chance and a payoff of $12 million (all guesses, so I'd give ranges). Expected value: X is 0.30 × $5M = $1.5M; Y is 0.15 × $12M = $1.8M. After test costs of $20,000 and $30,000: $1.48M and $1.77M. On paper Y wins, but the numbers are weak guesses.</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd add what the guesses don't capture. Learning speed: if I can test X in three weeks and Y in eight, I can resolve X sooner and move on if it fails, which has value. Evidence quality: if the success probability for Y rests on an analogy, I'd discount it. Reversibility and learning: which test also teaches something useful for the other idea or for the company? If the values are close and one test is much faster, I'd run the faster test first and keep the other in the queue; or, if the tests are cheap, run both small tests in parallel for a few weeks and decide on the results, which is often better than debating guesses.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence-adjusted comparison; expected value with caution; learning speed; parallel small tests.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the team has strong feelings about one? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd make the comparison explicit and agree the test and the threshold for both before starting, so that the decision follows the evidence. Attachment is normal; a shared rule protects the team's trust.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> When would you pursue the lower-expected-value idea? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;If it is much cheaper and faster to test, if it builds a capability the company needs, or if the higher-value idea has a hidden risk that the estimate ignores, such as regulation. Expected value is a guide, not the decision.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c26-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-01 · The idea funnel</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team starts with 40 ideas. Pass rates: screen 30%, scorecard 50%, tests 40%, build 50%. Hours per idea: screen 0.5, scorecard 4, test 25, prototype and pilot 150. Compute the number of ideas at each stage and the total hours, and compare with building all 40 at 1,500 hours each.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Funnel economics.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C26-02 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Screen: 40 ideas; 12 pass (30%). Scorecard: 12 ideas; 6 pass (50%). Tests: 6 ideas; about 2.4 pass (40%), so run tests on 6 and about 2 to 3 go on. Build: say 2 ideas enter prototype and pilot and 1 is built (50%). Hours: 40 × 0.5 = 20; 12 × 4 = 48; 6 × 25 = 150; 2 × 150 = 300; total 518 hours. Building all 40 at 1,500 hours: 60,000 hours. The funnel spends under 1% of that to find the one to build.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Show the funnel with the cost of a false kill: if 10% of killed ideas would have worked, estimate the loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Strict screens save hours and risk discarding good ideas; keep screen criteria about evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-02 · The riskiest-assumption map</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For the idea &quot;a subscription service that delivers a monthly box of plants chosen for your light and space,&quot; list six assumptions, rate impact (1 to 5) and uncertainty (1 to 5), find the riskiest and name the cheapest test.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Validation.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C26-04 and Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) People want a monthly plant delivery (value): impact 5, uncertainty 4, product 20. (2) They will pay about $30 a month (value): 5, 5, 25. (3) Plants survive delivery (feasibility): 4, 3, 12. (4) People can match plants to their light correctly from a questionnaire (usability): 4, 3, 12. (5) Retention beyond three months (value and viability): 5, 5, 25. (6) Unit economics work with shipping and losses (viability): 5, 4, 20. The riskiest are willingness to pay and retention (25 each). The cheapest test: a landing page with the price and a deposit (for willingness to pay), then a concierge pilot of three monthly boxes for 15 customers (for retention and economics). Thresholds in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a first test of the plant-survival assumption by shipping 20 plants to friends with a structured check.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Testing willingness first avoids spending on logistics; a feasibility check is cheap and can be done in parallel.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-03 · A smoke test sample size and thresholds</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You expect a sign-up rate near 3% and want it within ±2 points at 95% confidence. Compute the sample size. Set thresholds to proceed, persist and stop for sign-ups.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Test design.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> n ≈ 1.96² × p × (1 − p) ÷ δ².</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C26-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> n = 3.84 × 0.03 × 0.97 ÷ 0.0004 = about 280 visitors. Thresholds (set before the test): proceed at 3% or more sign-ups (about 8 of 280) together with a deposit rate of at least 0.5%; persist with a better test or audience between 1.5% and 3%; stop below 1.5%. Add a duration (two weeks) and a source rule: at least half the traffic from the target segment. Read the result with an interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Raise the sample to 700 to estimate the rate within ±1.3 points when the decision is costly.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A larger sample takes longer and costs more; set it by the cost of a wrong decision.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-04 · Read a smoke test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A smoke test with 900 visitors shows 27 sign-ups and 4 deposits. Thresholds set in advance: sign-ups at least 3%, deposits at least 0.5%. Compute rates and 95% intervals, compare with the thresholds and decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Reading results.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Standard error √(p(1 − p) ÷ n); interval ±1.96 standard errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Sign-ups: 27 ÷ 900 = 3.0%; standard error 0.57 points; interval 1.9% to 4.1%: the estimate meets the threshold exactly and the interval includes values below it: inconclusive. Deposits: 4 ÷ 900 = 0.44%; standard error 0.22 points; interval about 0.01% to 0.88%: below the 0.5% threshold and uncertain. Decision: persist with a better test (not proceed): increase the sample or improve the offer, and run a concierge test with paying customers. Do not lower the threshold to pass.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Check conversion by traffic source: if the target segment converts higher, the test audience was diluted.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Ambiguous results are common; a concierge test gives a stronger signal for the cost.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-05 · Concierge economics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A concierge test sells a service at $40 a week. Delivery takes 2.5 hours of labor at $20 an hour plus $4 of materials. Compute the margin per customer-week, and the hours needed for a 50% margin. Over 10 customers for 8 weeks, what does learning cost?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Economics of a manual test.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Cost: 2.5 × $20 + $4 = $54; margin: $40 − $54 = −$14 per customer-week. For a 50% margin cost must be at most $20: labor at most $16, so 0.8 hours. Learning cost over 10 customers for 8 weeks: 80 customer-weeks × $14 = $1,120. Next: find the steps that take the most time and automate those first; set a kill criterion: stop if automation cannot get time below about 1 hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test a higher price ($55): margin of $1; check whether willingness to pay allows it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A higher price tests willingness to pay and may reduce the number of customers.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-06 · Score and compare</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Weights: severity 25%, reach 15%, willingness to pay 15%, right to win 15%, feasibility 10%, differentiation 10%, fit 10%. Idea P scores 4, 3, 3, 3, 4, 3, 3 with strong evidence (factor 1.0). Idea Q scores 3, 4, 4, 2, 3, 4, 2 with weak evidence (factor 0.6). Compute the weighted and adjusted scores and say what to test.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Scorecard.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C26-03 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> P: 0.25 × 4 + 0.15 × 3 + 0.15 × 3 + 0.15 × 3 + 0.10 × 4 + 0.10 × 3 + 0.10 × 3 = 3.35; adjusted 3.35. Q: 0.25 × 3 + 0.15 × 4 + 0.15 × 4 + 0.15 × 2 + 0.10 × 3 + 0.10 × 4 + 0.10 × 2 = 3.15; adjusted 3.15 × 0.6 = 1.89. P ranks first on both measures, and its lead grows when evidence is considered. Q's weak evidence means its score is mostly hope; to compare fairly, cheap tests could raise the evidence quality (for instance, willingness to pay and right to win). Test P's weakest scores first and run one cheap test on Q's highest score (willingness to pay, 4) to see whether it holds.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use a confidence factor of 0.8 for Q and show that P still leads (2.52 against 3.35).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The confidence factor is a judgment; show it transparently.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-07 · Write kill criteria</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For a new grocery-delivery feature in a messaging app, write kill, persist and pivot criteria, with measures, thresholds and a time box.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Decision rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C26-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Measures: the share of weekly active users in the pilot city who place an order within eight weeks (adoption); repeat orders within 30 days; orders per active user; delivery issues per 100 orders as a guardrail. <em>Proceed</em> if at least 8% adopt, at least 40% of them order again within 30 days, and issues are under 5 per 100. <em>Persist with a better test</em> if adoption is 4% to 8% and repeat is above 30%, with a specific fix to test in four weeks. <em>Kill</em> if adoption is under 4% after eight weeks or repeat orders are under 20%. <em>Pivot signals:</em> strong adoption and repeat in one segment (for example, families with young children) and weak elsewhere: test a narrower proposition. Time box: eight weeks for the pilot, reviewed at four. Write the criteria before launching the pilot and date them.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add an economics criterion: stop if contribution per order stays negative after volume doubles.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Pre-set criteria can feel rigid; revise them only by an agreed process, not after results.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c26-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C26-08 · Is it product-market fit?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Cohort retention: week 1, 35%; week 4, 10%; week 8, 5%; week 12, 2%. A survey finds 18% would be very disappointed without the product. Acquisition is 90% paid. What do you conclude and do?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Traction signals.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C26-07 and IQ-C26-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The retention curve keeps falling toward zero rather than flattening, so no core is retained. The survey figure (18%) is well below the commonly cited 40% <code>[V: Sean Ellis survey and 40% benchmark, as described in Rahul Vohra's First Round article]</code> and below a 30% kill line. Acquisition is mostly paid, so growth would stop with spend. These signals indicate no fit. Before killing: read the data by segment (is any segment above, say, 35% very disappointed or retaining above 15%?), check whether the audience is right, and check the onboarding: could a fixable activation problem explain the early drop? If no segment shows pull, kill or restart with a new problem; do not scale or buy more traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Interview the retained 2% to see whether they use the product for a different purpose than designed, which could suggest a pivot.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Stopping early saves money and risks missing a late bloomer; a time-boxed segment search balances them.</p>\n<span id=\"c26-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: evaluate and design tests.</em> Use IQ-C26-01, IQ-C26-02, IQ-C26-03, EX-C26-01, EX-C26-02 and EX-C26-03. For three ideas of your own, fill in a scorecard with evidence quality and an assumption map, and write a test card with thresholds.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: read results and decide.</em> Use IQ-C26-04, IQ-C26-05, IQ-C26-06, IQ-C26-08, EX-C26-04, EX-C26-05, EX-C26-07 and EX-C26-08. Compute intervals by hand, write kill criteria for a real project, and practice the 25-minute answer aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;5% signed up, is that good?&quot; and &quot;Isn't the waiting list proof?&quot; at random, and to ask for the threshold you set before the test.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I start from the problem, the user and the evidence?</li>\n<li>Did I score with criteria and evidence quality?</li>\n<li>Did I name the riskiest assumption by impact and uncertainty?</li>\n<li>Is my test the cheapest that can fail, with a threshold and sample size set in advance?</li>\n<li>Did I read results with an interval, and consider audience and commitment?</li>\n<li>Did I set kill, persist and pivot criteria before the test?</li>\n<li>Did I check sunk cost and read data by segment?</li>\n<li>Did I distinguish real traction from false signals?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the prompt, the assumption you tested, the threshold, your score and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c26-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on a real idea over the next three weeks. If you have no new product at work, use a feature bet or a side project.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Write down five problems you have observed (with evidence) and three ideas for each. Place them on the opportunity map (FW-C26-01). Choose the strongest three and score them (FW-C26-03), marking evidence quality.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> For the top idea, build the riskiest-assumption map (FW-C26-04) and write a test card (FW-C26-05) with the measure, the threshold, the sample size and the decision rule. Ask a colleague to attack the test: can it fail?</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run the cheapest test: a landing page with a price, five customer interviews with a commitment question, or a manual version for three customers. Read the result with an interval and the audience in mind.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Decide with the pre-set criteria (FW-C26-06): kill, persist or pivot; write the decision and the next test, with a time box. Record what you learned.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the scorecard with evidence quality (a time you evaluated an idea rigorously), the test card and result (a time you validated cheaply), and the decision record (a time you decided on evidence, including to stop). Keep numbers real and credit partners.</p>\n<span id=\"c26-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c26-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Ideas start from observed problems; classify them (improve the core, new market, adjacent product, diversification; painkiller or vitamin), generate many, and pass them through a funnel that spends the fewest hours on the most that will fail.</li>\n<li>Evaluate with explicit criteria and the quality of the evidence behind each score; evidence-adjusted ranking reflects what you know, and the scorecard tells you what to test, not what to build.</li>\n<li>Name the riskiest assumption (high impact, high uncertainty, usually that people will pay) and test it with the cheapest experiment that can fail (smoke test, concierge, Wizard-of-Oz, prototype), with the measure, threshold and sample size set in advance; read results with intervals and watch audience and commitment bias.</li>\n<li>Write kill, persist and pivot criteria before the test, decide by them and by segment data, treat a pivot as a new hypothesis with its own criteria and time box, and ignore sunk cost.</li>\n<li>Trust traction that shows in retention that flattens, a strong &quot;very disappointed&quot; share, organic growth and willingness to pay by segment, and distrust waiting lists, launch spikes, paid dependence and averages; what changes from zero to one to ten is the metrics, the team, the quality bar and the go-to-market.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c26-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Eric Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em> (2011), the source of the minimum viable product vocabulary and the Dropbox account in CS-C26-02 (read as TechCrunch's 19 October 2011 excerpt) <code>[V: TechCrunch excerpt of The Lean Startup, 19 October 2011]</code>.</li>\n<li>The Hacker News thread of 4 April 2007 (&quot;My YC app: Dropbox - Throw away your USB drive&quot;), the First Round Review account of Drew Houston's talk, an interview with Houston on startups.com and TechCrunch's 2011 recap of his Founder Stories interview. Sources for CS-C26-02 (confirmed in October 2026).</li>\n<li>Rob Fitzpatrick, <em>The Mom Test</em> (2013), on interviewing for real evidence of problems <code>[V: CreateSpace Independent Publishing Platform, 10 September 2013, ISBN 9781492180746]</code>.</li>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2017), on product discovery and the four risks <code>[V: 2nd edition, Wiley, December 2017, ISBN 9781119387503; the discussion of the four risks was not opened]</code>.</li>\n<li>Alberto Savoia, <em>The Right It</em> (2019), on pretotyping and testing market demand <code>[V: HarperOne, 2019, ISBN 9780062884657]</code>.</li>\n<li>Sean Ellis's work on the &quot;very disappointed&quot; survey and product-market fit, and the Superhuman account in C06 <code>[V: Sean Ellis survey; Vohra, First Round Review]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c27\"></span>\n<h2>C27 — Domain Primers</h2>\n<span id=\"c27-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Design a trust program for a marketplace with third-party sellers.&quot; &quot;Authorization rates on card payments fell 1.2 points; how do you diagnose it?&quot; &quot;Who are the buyer, the user and the administrator of an enterprise product, and why does it matter?&quot; &quot;How does an ad auction pick an ad and set its price?&quot; Interviewers often set a product question inside a domain (a marketplace, a payments product, enterprise software, a safety problem) and listen for whether you understand what makes that domain different: the actors, the constraints, the metrics, the risks and the tradeoffs. This chapter gives a primer for nine domains, each with what is distinctive, the concepts to know, the questions to expect and a metric tree, so that you can reason in any of them without starting from nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: product-sense, strategy and analytical rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, where the prompt names a domain <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job, a PM moving to a new domain faces the same question: what do I need to learn first?</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you adapt your general method to the domain <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for the right actors and incentives; the domain's specific risks (fraud, abuse, regulation, long sales cycles, hardware that cannot be patched); metrics that fit the domain; and tradeoffs named in the domain's own terms.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You identify the actors in the domain and what each wants.</li>\n<li>You name the constraint that makes the domain different (for example, trust, compliance, adversaries, long cycles or regulation).</li>\n<li>You use the domain's own vocabulary correctly and sparingly.</li>\n<li>You build a metric tree that fits the domain and has guardrails.</li>\n<li>You state the tradeoff the domain forces and take a position.</li>\n<li>You say what you would learn first if the domain were new to you.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end domain answers: a consumer-app answer to an enterprise question, ignoring fraud and compliance in payments, treating safety as a content-filter problem, measuring a marketplace by sign-ups, copying a consumer growth tactic into a regulated domain, and jargon without understanding.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<p>Each primer follows a shape (FW-C27-01): <em>what is distinctive; the actors and what they want; key concepts; metrics; typical tradeoffs and questions.</em> Domain terms are defined where first needed.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-1-marketplace-liquidity-take-rate-trust\"></span>\n<h4>C27.1 Marketplace: liquidity, take rate, trust</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> A marketplace connects two sides, and its value depends on both being present and finding each other (C19 covered two-sided markets, take rates and cold start).</p>\n<p><strong>Actors.</strong> Buyers (demand), sellers or suppliers (supply), the platform, and often third parties (payments, delivery).</p>\n<p><strong>Liquidity.</strong> <strong>Liquidity</strong> is the likelihood that a participant on one side finds what they want from the other side quickly: for buyers, that a search ends in a satisfying purchase; for sellers, that their listing sells or is used. It is the central health measure. Measures <code>Inferred</code>: the share of searches that show at least one available, relevant result; search-to-purchase conversion; time to first match; and supplier utilization (the share of capacity that is booked).</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>Supply and demand balance:</em> adding supply without demand leaves sellers idle and drives them away; adding demand without supply produces empty searches and drives buyers away; the binding side changes by market and by time (Worked example 1). <em>Take rate:</em> the fee share (C19); too high pushes users to transact off-platform (<strong>leakage</strong>, the loss of transactions that move off the platform after matching, C19 disintermediation). <em>Trust:</em> ratings, verification, guarantees and dispute resolution matter more than features when strangers transact; a single bad incident can damage both sides. <em>Matching and search ranking:</em> ranking should favor transactions likely to succeed and to be good for both sides, not only those with the highest fee.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Gross merchandise value (GMV) = active buyers × orders per buyer × average order value; active buyers depend on new and returning buyers, which depend on search success and trust; supply depends on new sellers, active sellers and their utilization; revenue = GMV × take rate; guardrails: dispute rate, refund rate, seller churn and quality ratings.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Quality versus quantity of supply; fees versus leakage; growth in one city versus depth in a few; speed of onboarding versus verification. &quot;How would you grow supply in a new city?&quot; &quot;How would you reduce cancellations?&quot; &quot;Design trust for sellers you do not know.&quot;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how to grow a new local-services marketplace, a candidate proposes a seller sign-up drive and reports success as the number of sellers who joined. The interviewer asks what share of buyer requests get a reply within an hour. The candidate has measured supply and not liquidity: in the cities with the most new sellers, many requests still go unanswered because the sellers offer the wrong services. A better start: &quot;I'd measure the share of requests that get a reply, find where they go unfilled, and add supply only there.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>When not to use a domain primer.</strong> Do not force a domain label onto a problem that is general (a product-sense question about a to-do list needs the general method, not marketplace vocabulary), and do not recite jargon to signal expertise; use the domain's terms only where they change the answer.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-2-enterprise-software-buyers-versus-users-sales-cycles\"></span>\n<h4>C27.2 Enterprise software: buyers versus users, sales cycles</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> The person who pays is often not the person who uses the product, and the purchase involves many people, security review and contracts.</p>\n<p><strong>Actors.</strong> The <em>economic buyer</em> (holds the budget), the <em>champion</em> (wants it and advocates), the <em>end users</em>, the <em>administrators</em> (configure and secure it), <em>IT and security</em> (approve or block), <em>procurement and legal</em>, and the vendor's sales and customer-success teams. A <em>buying committee</em> (a common term in enterprise sales) is the group of such people whose agreement the deal needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> A <strong>sales cycle</strong> is the time from first contact to a signed contract, often months in enterprise sales, shaped by the buying committee, security review and budget cycles. <em>Land and expand:</em> start with a small team or product and grow within the customer. <em>Product-led entry</em> (C20.5) lets users adopt before the purchase and sales follow; many products blend both. <em>Requirements that decide deals but are not features:</em> single sign-on, role-based access, audit logs, data residency, compliance reports and certifications (such as a SOC 2 report, which an independent auditor issues against the AICPA Trust Services Criteria <code>[V: AICPA: SOC 2 is an attestation report by an independent CPA firm against the Trust Services Criteria (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy)]</code>), service-level agreements, admin controls and support. <strong>Net revenue retention</strong> (NRR; also net dollar retention) is the revenue from a cohort of customers a year later, including expansion and net of churn and downgrades, as a percentage of their revenue at the start; above 100% means existing customers grow even without new customers. <em>Customization versus product:</em> a feature built for one large customer can distort the roadmap (C32.7 on saying no).</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Revenue = customers × annual contract value (ACV); new customers = pipeline × win rate; pipeline depends on lead sources and cycle time; expansion = seats and products per customer; retention = logo churn and NRR; product health = activation per account, seat utilization and weekly active users per account; guardrails: support load, security incidents, time to resolve.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Buyer value versus user love; roadmap from strategy versus from customers; seat-based versus usage-based pricing (C20.3); product-led versus sales-led. &quot;How would you win a large customer from a competitor?&quot; &quot;How would you prioritize between a feature for ten users and one for a customer paying a million?&quot;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how to grow a new enterprise chat product, a candidate says: &quot;Make it viral: add invitations and rewards so employees spread it.&quot; The interviewer asks who signs the contract and what the company's security team will require. The candidate has applied a consumer growth tactic and has not mentioned the buyer, the buying committee, security review or the sales cycle. A better start: &quot;Usage can spread bottom-up, and the deal is signed by a buyer who needs single sign-on, access control, audit logs and compliance; so I'd design for both: easy team adoption and the requirements that let a large customer say yes.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-3-fintech-trust-compliance-payments\"></span>\n<h4>C27.3 Fintech: trust, compliance, payments</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> Money movement is regulated, irreversible in practice, a target for fraud, and unforgiving of errors; trust and reliability are the product.</p>\n<p><strong>Actors.</strong> Consumers and merchants, banks (issuers and acquirers), card networks, regulators, and the fintech's partners.</p>\n<p><strong>The payment flow (simplified).</strong> A card payment is <em>authorized</em> (the issuer approves or declines), <em>captured</em> (the merchant claims the funds) and <em>settled</em> (money moves to the merchant, often days later), with possible <em>refunds</em> and <em>chargebacks</em>. A <strong>chargeback</strong> is a reversal of a card payment forced by the cardholder's bank after a dispute, usually accompanied by a fee, and a main cost of fraud. <em>Ledgers</em> record every movement and must balance exactly.</p>\n<p><strong>Compliance.</strong> <strong>Know your customer</strong> (KYC) is the process of verifying a customer's identity (and, for businesses, ownership and activity), required by financial regulation; related obligations include anti-money-laundering monitoring and sanctions screening <code>[V: RBI Master Direction on KYC (2016, amended 2023 and 2024), aligned with FATF standards]</code>. Compliance shapes the product: onboarding steps, limits, holds and reviews, and these steps cost conversion.</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>Authorization rate</em> (the share of payment attempts approved) is a core metric: small changes move revenue (C20; Worked example 3). <em>Fraud and false declines:</em> blocking fraud and approving good customers trade off (C23.3, C25). <em>Reliability:</em> an outage stops money movement; availability and idempotency matter (C21, C22). <em>Partners and rails:</em> the product depends on banks and networks whose rules change.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Net revenue = attempts × authorization rate × average amount × take rate − fraud and chargeback losses − processing cost; guardrails: fraud rate, chargeback rate, false-decline rate, time to settle, onboarding completion and compliance incident rate.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Payments in India are shaped by real-time bank-to-bank transfers through the Unified Payments Interface (UPI), which is widely used for consumer and small-merchant payments, with rules and standards set by the central bank and the payments body <code>[V: RBI and NPCI; merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026]</code>. Recurring payments and subscriptions follow rules on mandates and customer authentication, payment aggregators are regulated (non-bank aggregators need RBI authorization), and storage of payment data is subject to localization requirements <code>[V: RBI payment aggregator directions, 2025; RBI storage directive FAQ]</code>; recurring-payment mandates follow the RBI's e-mandate framework, with authentication waived up to 15,000 rupees and up to 1,00,000 rupees for some categories <code>[V: RBI circular RBI/2023-24/88 of 12 December 2023; e-mandate framework of August 2019; the same limits are restated in RBI's 2026 consolidated directions on e-mandates (RBI/DPSS/2026-27/396), as reported by TaxGuru and not opened in full]</code>. Customer identity checks commonly use national digital identity and video verification within regulatory limits <code>[V: RBI Master Direction on KYC, as amended in 2023 and 2024]</code>, and personal data is governed by the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, in phases <code>[V: law-firm summaries of the DPDP Rules]</code>. For a PM the consequences are to design around bank-account rails and mandates, to treat regulatory change as a roadmap input, and to learn the local rules before copying a card-based design.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Friction versus risk; speed of onboarding versus compliance; reliability versus new features; fees versus conversion. &quot;Authorization rates fell; why?&quot; &quot;Design onboarding for a lending product.&quot; &quot;How would you reduce chargebacks?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-4-trust-and-safety-adversaries-and-tradeoffs\"></span>\n<h4>C27.4 Trust and safety: adversaries and tradeoffs</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> <strong>Trust and safety</strong> is the work of protecting users and the platform from abuse, fraud, harassment, harmful content and manipulation, through policy, product design, detection and enforcement. Its defining feature is an <em>adversary who adapts</em> (C22.4 on abuse cases), and its defining difficulty is that every decision harms someone: a false removal silences a legitimate user; a missed violation harms others.</p>\n<p><strong>Actors.</strong> Users (including victims and abusers), reviewers (human moderators), policy teams, legal, engineers and researchers, regulators and the public.</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>Policy first:</em> write what is not allowed and why, before detection. <em>Layers</em> (C25.4): design that prevents abuse, automated detection, human review, user reports, appeals and enforcement. <em>Precision and recall</em> (C23.3): a stricter classifier catches more and wrongly removes more; thresholds are policy decisions with costs for each error. <strong>Prevalence</strong> is the estimated share of views (or content) that violate a policy, measured by sampling and review: it measures what users actually see, unlike counts of removals, which can rise because detection improved. <em>Other measures:</em> proactive detection rate (the share of violations found before users report), time to action, appeal rate and overturn rate (a signal of false removals), and repeat-offender rates. <em>Human review:</em> capacity, quality, reviewer wellbeing and consistency. <em>Appeals and transparency:</em> fairness requires a way to contest decisions. <em>Adaptation:</em> adversaries change tactics, so measure over time and test with red teams (C25.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Harm to users ≈ prevalence × exposure; prevalence falls through prevention, detection and enforcement; costs: false removals (appeal overturn rate), reviewer load and delay; guardrails: legitimate-user friction, regional and language coverage, and fairness across groups.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Speed versus accuracy; automation versus human judgment; speech versus harm; global policy versus local norms; privacy versus detection. &quot;Design the safety approach for a new live-video feature.&quot; &quot;Reduce harassment without driving away good users.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-5-advertising-and-monetization-advertiser-versus-user-te\"></span>\n<h4>C27.5 Advertising and monetization: advertiser versus user tension, auction basics at PM depth</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> The people who use the product are not the ones who pay for it; the advertiser pays and the user supplies attention, and the product must serve both without breaking trust.</p>\n<p><strong>Actors.</strong> Users, advertisers (and agencies), publishers or the platform, and measurement partners.</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>Ad load</em> (how many ads) trades revenue against experience and retention. An <strong>ad auction</strong> is the mechanism by which an advertising platform decides, each time an ad slot is available, which advertisers' ads to show and at what price, typically ranking advertisers by their bid combined with the predicted chance that the user will respond (and sometimes ad quality), and charging the winner the minimum needed to keep its position, a rule akin to a second-price auction (FW-C27-06; Worked example 5). Ranking by bid times predicted response rewards relevant ads, protects users from irrelevant ones and raises platform revenue per impression. <em>Pricing models:</em> cost per thousand impressions, per click, per action. <em>Targeting and measurement:</em> better targeting raises value; privacy rules and platform changes (such as limits on cross-app tracking) reduce signal and change advertiser results <code>[V: Apple App Tracking Transparency, iOS 14.5]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Revenue = impressions × revenue per thousand impressions (RPM); RPM = bids × predicted response × auction pressure (number of competing bidders); impressions = users × sessions × ad slots per session (ad load); advertiser value: return on spend and repeat spend; guardrails: user retention, satisfaction, complaints about ads, and ad quality (policy violations).</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Ad load versus retention; relevance versus privacy; short-term revenue versus advertiser trust. &quot;How would you add ads to a product that has none?&quot; &quot;Explain how an auction chooses an ad.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-6-consumer-devices-and-hardware-software-products-includ\"></span>\n<h4>C27.6 Consumer devices and hardware-software products, including integrated ecosystems (primer)</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> Hardware cannot be patched like software, cycles are long, costs are fixed once manufacturing starts, and the product spans hardware, software and services.</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>Bill of materials and margin:</em> each unit carries cost; price sets margin (C20.3). <em>Long lead times:</em> decisions about components are made a year or more before launch; supply chains and manufacturing capacity constrain volume. <em>Launch once:</em> a hardware defect becomes returns, recalls and reputation (C17.3 and the Note 7 case in C17); testing is more extensive and rollouts less reversible. <em>Hardware-software co-design:</em> the experience depends on both, and over-the-air updates can improve software but not parts. <em>Ecosystem effects:</em> devices that work better together, accessories, a store and services increase value and switching costs (C18, C19, C24.5 on on-device processing). <em>Services attach</em> (C20.3): the recurring contribution of services on a device base.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Revenue = units × price + services attach × services revenue; units = demand × supply; margin = price − unit cost − returns; guardrails: return and defect rates, support contacts, battery and reliability complaints, and privacy incidents; engagement: daily use, feature adoption and repeat purchase.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Features versus cost and schedule; openness versus integration; premium margin versus volume. &quot;Price a new device and its subscription.&quot; &quot;A defect affects 0.5% of units; what do you do?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-7-infrastructure-and-internal-platform-products-customer\"></span>\n<h4>C27.7 Infrastructure and internal-platform products: customers are other teams (primer)</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> An <strong>internal platform</strong> is a product (a service, tool or set of interfaces) whose customers are other teams within the same company, who often cannot easily go elsewhere and cannot be forced to be delighted. The same product principles apply, and the usual levers (price, marketing) do not.</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>Customers and adoption:</em> internal teams adopt a platform when it saves them time or risk; mandates produce compliance and resentment; adoption is the primary success measure. <em>Developer experience</em> (C19.3): time to first successful use, documentation, clarity of errors. <em>Reliability and service levels</em> (C21): internal customers depend on service-level objectives and notice outages. <em>Roadmap negotiation:</em> many teams ask for features, and the PM must prioritize by company-wide value and say no (C32.7). <em>Deprecation and migration:</em> removing old interfaces is costly for customers; plan, communicate and help migrate. <em>Platform versus product tension:</em> a platform serves many use cases and can become slow to change.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Value = adoption × impact per adopter; adoption = teams onboarded × share of workload moved; impact = time saved, incidents avoided or cost saved; guardrails: reliability (availability, latency), support load, customer satisfaction, and migration progress.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Standardization versus flexibility; mandate versus persuasion; one platform versus several. &quot;Adoption of an internal tool is low; what do you do?&quot; &quot;How would you prioritize requests from forty teams?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-8-growth-product-management\"></span>\n<h4>C27.8 Growth product management</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> Growth PMs own the movement of users through acquisition, activation, retention, referral and revenue, using experiments (C14) and loops (C20.5).</p>\n<p><strong>Concepts.</strong> <em>The funnel and loops</em> (C05, C20.5): find the biggest drop, and look for loops that compound. <em>Activation:</em> the first moment of value is often the highest-leverage point (C05). <em>Experimentation at volume:</em> many small tests, guardrails on retention and trust, and care about novelty and interference (C14). <em>Ethics:</em> growth tactics that trick users (hidden costs, forced sharing, pre-checked boxes, difficult cancellation) are dark patterns (C10.10) and carry legal and trust costs. <em>Short term versus long term:</em> a tactic that raises sign-ups and lowers retention reduces value. <em>Unit economics</em> (C20.7): acquisition must pay back.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Weekly active users = new + resurrected + retained; new users = visitors × sign-up rate × activation; retained = prior users × retention; revenue = active users × conversion × revenue per paying user; guardrails: long-term retention, complaints and refunds, cost of acquisition.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical tradeoffs and questions.</strong> Growth versus quality of users; experiments versus coherence; incentives versus organic growth. &quot;Weekly active users grew 12% but retention fell; why, and what do you do?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-9-regulated-domains-health-and-education-notes\"></span>\n<h4>C27.9 Regulated domains: health and education notes</h4>\n<p><strong>What is distinctive.</strong> In health and education, outcomes matter more than engagement, evidence standards are higher, privacy rules are stricter and mistakes harm vulnerable people. Cycles are slow and review is mandatory.</p>\n<p><strong>Health notes.</strong> <em>Safety and efficacy:</em> a product that diagnoses or treats may be a regulated medical device, with clinical validation and regulatory approval <code>[V: FDA, EU MDR and India's Medical Devices Rules 2017 regulate software with a medical purpose]</code>. <em>Privacy:</em> health data is sensitive and tightly regulated (for example, HIPAA in the United States, which applies to covered entities such as health plans, clearinghouses and providers, and to their business associates, but not to every health app; and data protection law elsewhere) <code>[V: HHS: HIPAA Privacy Rule and Security Rule apply to covered entities and business associates]</code>. <em>Clinical workflow:</em> products that disrupt clinicians' work fail; liability and who is responsible for a decision matter. <em>Outcomes:</em> improvement in health measures, adherence and safety events, not time in app. <em>AI in health</em> (C24, C25) raises evaluation, error cost and human-in-the-loop questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Education notes.</strong> <em>Learning outcomes:</em> evidence that learners learn, not completion or engagement. <em>Minors:</em> protections for children's data and advertising, with regional rules <code>[V: children's consent ages differ: GDPR Article 8 (16, lowerable to 13 by member states), US COPPA (under 13), India DPDP Act (under 18)]</code>. <em>Users and buyers differ:</em> learners, teachers, parents and institutions. <em>Equity and access:</em> connectivity and devices vary (C09).</p>\n<p><strong>Common to both:</strong> consult domain experts and ethics reviewers early; design evaluation around outcomes; expect slower launches and careful claims; avoid applying consumer growth tactics unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>Metric tree.</strong> Value = reach × adherence × outcome improvement; guardrails: safety events, privacy incidents, equity of access, and the evidence quality behind claims.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-c27-10-domain-metric-trees\"></span>\n<h4>C27.10 Domain metric trees</h4>\n<p>A metric tree breaks a goal into drivers you can act on (C05). The nine trees above, in one view (FW-C27-07):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Top metric</th>\n<th>First-level drivers</th>\n<th>Key guardrails</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Marketplace</td>\n<td>GMV (and revenue = GMV × take rate)</td>\n<td>Active buyers × orders × order value; supply × utilization; liquidity</td>\n<td>Disputes, refunds, seller churn, ratings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise software</td>\n<td>Net revenue (ACV × customers; NRR)</td>\n<td>Pipeline × win rate; expansion; retention</td>\n<td>Support load, security incidents, time to resolve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fintech and payments</td>\n<td>Net revenue after losses</td>\n<td>Attempts × authorization rate × amount × take rate; losses</td>\n<td>Fraud, chargebacks, false declines, compliance incidents, uptime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trust and safety</td>\n<td>Harm to users (prevalence × exposure)</td>\n<td>Prevention, detection, enforcement</td>\n<td>Appeal overturn rate, legitimate-user friction, language and fairness coverage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advertising</td>\n<td>Revenue (impressions × RPM)</td>\n<td>Auction pressure, predicted response, ad load</td>\n<td>Retention, satisfaction, ad quality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Devices</td>\n<td>Units × price + services</td>\n<td>Demand, supply, attach</td>\n<td>Returns, defects, support, privacy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal platform</td>\n<td>Adoption × impact</td>\n<td>Teams onboarded, workload moved</td>\n<td>Reliability, support load, satisfaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Growth</td>\n<td>Weekly active users and revenue</td>\n<td>New, resurrected, retained; conversion</td>\n<td>Long-term retention, complaints, acquisition payback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Health and education</td>\n<td>Outcome improvement × reach</td>\n<td>Adherence, access</td>\n<td>Safety events, privacy incidents, equity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Use.</strong> In an interview, draw the tree for the named domain in under two minutes, say which driver you would investigate first, and name one guardrail.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Seven frameworks, set out below. The metric card (FW-C05-01), the two-sided market and governance tools (C19), the failure-mode table (FW-C22-06), the abuse-case pass (FW-C22-08), the threshold by cost (FW-C23-02), the pricing worksheet (FW-C20-03) and the unit-economics sheet (FW-C20-06) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-01 · The domain primer card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a domain named in a prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> what is distinctive; the actors and what each wants; the key concepts and constraints; the metric tree with guardrails; the typical tradeoffs; and what you would learn first if the domain were new.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the general method (users, problem, options, metrics, tradeoffs) still applies.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a card gives orientation and not depth; follow the interviewer's lead.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a consumer-app answer in an enterprise or regulated domain; jargon without understanding.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> all five worked examples; C27.10.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What is distinctive</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Actors and wants</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key concepts and constraints</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric tree and guardrails</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical tradeoffs</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn first</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-02 · The marketplace liquidity check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a market (city or category) with searches, available supply, conversions and utilization.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the share of searches with at least one available result; conversion given results; the booking rate; supplier utilization; the binding side (supply or demand); the lever (add supply, add demand, improve matching); and the effect of the lever with a guardrail on utilization.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> metrics are measured per market and per week.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> adding supply without demand strands suppliers; adding demand without supply wastes acquisition.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> measuring sign-ups; averaging across markets.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formula: <strong>booking rate = share of searches with available results × conversion given results.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-03 · The enterprise buying map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a target customer organization and a product.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the economic buyer, champion, users, administrators, IT and security, procurement and legal; each one's concern; the stages and length of the sales cycle; the non-feature requirements (single sign-on, access control, audit logs, compliance, service levels); and a plan to win each.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the buyer and the user differ.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> serving the buyer's checklist can slow the product loved by users.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> ignoring security review; a single contact; one-off customization.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Concern</th>\n<th>Evidence they need</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-04 · The payments flow and risk map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a payment product.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the flow (authorize, capture, settle, refund, dispute); where fraud, decline, error and delay occur; the metrics (authorization rate, fraud rate, chargeback rate, false-decline rate, time to settle); the compliance steps (identity checks, monitoring) and their conversion cost; the reliability needs; and a cost comparison of tighter and looser fraud screening.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> a false decline has a cost beyond the lost margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> friction versus risk; speed versus review.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> ignoring false declines; ignoring partner rules; counting only fraud.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-05 · The trust and safety decision frame · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a harm and a surface.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the policy; the adversaries and how they adapt; the layers (prevent, detect, review, enforce, appeal); the prevalence estimate and target; precision and recall at the chosen threshold and the cost of each error; human-review capacity; the appeals and overturn measure; and fairness and coverage checks across languages and groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> prevalence is sampled and reviewed, not inferred from removals.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a lower prevalence usually costs more false removals and more reviewers.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> measuring removals; one threshold for all harms; no appeals.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formula: <strong>prevalence = violating views ÷ total views.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-06 · The ad-auction worksheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> bidders with bids and predicted response rates (and a quality factor if used).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> each bidder's score (bid × predicted response); the ranking; the winner's price (the minimum bid that would keep its position); revenue per thousand impressions at bid and at price; and the effect of weighting by predicted response compared with ranking by bid alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> predicted response rates are reasonably calibrated (C23).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> weighting by quality protects users and may lower revenue in the short run.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> ranking by bid alone; ignoring ad load and retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formula: <strong>score = bid × predicted response; winner's price per click = next-highest score ÷ the winner's predicted response.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c27-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C27-07 · The domain metric tree · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a domain and a goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a tree with the top metric, first-level drivers, the driver to investigate first and the reason, and at least one guardrail; drawn in under two minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> drivers can be measured and acted on (C05).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a broad tree shows range; a deep one shows judgment.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a tree with no guardrail; a tree of vanity metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> C27.10 and IQ-C27-09.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c27-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c27-the-design-or-improve-a-product-in-domain-x-answer-for-30-mi\"></span>\n<h4>The &quot;design or improve a product in domain X&quot; answer, for 30 minutes</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain orientation</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The actors and what each wants; the constraint that makes the domain different</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The user and problem</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The segment and the problem, inside the domain's rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options and choice</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Three options, one chosen with reasons that use the domain's terms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric tree and guardrails</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The domain tree; the first driver to examine; guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks and tradeoffs</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The domain's tradeoff, fraud, abuse, compliance or adoption risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test and rollout</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A staged plan and a decision rule</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If you do not know the domain, say so in one sentence and ask two orienting questions (who pays, who is at risk); then proceed from the general method.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> &quot;In a marketplace the binding constraint is usually one side; I'd first check what share of searches show an available result, because that tells me whether supply or demand is short.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> &quot;So I'd add supply in the market where only 40% of searches have an available listing, test it for four weeks, and watch supplier utilization so we don't strand hosts.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-type-a-marketplace-question\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a marketplace question</h4>\n<p>Liquidity check, binding side, trust, take rate and leakage.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-type-an-enterprise-product-question\"></span>\n<h4>Type: an enterprise product question</h4>\n<p>Buying map, buyer versus user, non-feature requirements, land and expand, NRR.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-type-a-payments-or-fintech-question\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a payments or fintech question</h4>\n<p>Flow, authorization versus fraud, compliance, reliability, partner rules.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-type-a-safety-or-abuse-question\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a safety or abuse question</h4>\n<p>Adversaries, layers, prevalence, thresholds with error costs, appeals.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-type-advertising-devices-platforms-growth-or-regulated-domai\"></span>\n<h4>Type: advertising, devices, platforms, growth or regulated domains</h4>\n<p>The relevant primer: auction mechanics; hardware constraints; internal adoption; funnel and loops with guardrails; outcomes and evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How is this different from a consumer app?&quot;</td>\n<td>Domain awareness</td>\n<td>Name the actors and the constraint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Who is the customer?&quot;</td>\n<td>Buyer versus user</td>\n<td>Separate buyer, user and administrator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if fraud rises?&quot;</td>\n<td>Risk tradeoff</td>\n<td>Compare tighter and looser screening with costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How do you know it's working?&quot;</td>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>The tree, the first driver and a guardrail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Won't bad actors adapt?&quot;</td>\n<td>Adversaries</td>\n<td>Layers, monitoring, red teams</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What would you learn first?&quot;</td>\n<td>Humility</td>\n<td>Two orienting questions and one data check</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c27-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the markets, products and figures are invented (invented), and the arithmetic was computed. Companies are settings only.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-worked-example-1-which-side-of-the-marketplace-is-short-illu\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Which side of the marketplace is short? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C27-02).</strong> A home-sharing marketplace compares two cities for the week (invented):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>City A</th>\n<th>City B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Listings (hosts)</td>\n<td>400</td>\n<td>150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Searches</td>\n<td>3,000</td>\n<td>2,500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share of searches with at least one available result</td>\n<td>85%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conversion given a result</td>\n<td>4.7%</td>\n<td>3.75%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Booking rate (product of the last two rows)</td>\n<td>4.0%</td>\n<td>1.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bookings</td>\n<td>120</td>\n<td>37.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bookings per host per week</td>\n<td>0.30</td>\n<td>0.25</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Read it.</strong> Conversion given a result is similar (4.7% and 3.75%): once a guest sees an available listing, they book at similar rates. The difference is availability: in City B, 60% of searches end with nothing available. So City B is supply-constrained, and City A is balanced. Host utilization in B (0.25 bookings a week) is close to A's (0.30), so hosts in B are not starved; there is unmet demand.</p>\n<p><strong>The lever.</strong> Add supply in B. Suppose adding 100 hosts raises availability from 40% to 70% and conversion stays at 3.75%: booking rate 0.70 × 0.0375 = 2.6%, bookings 2,500 × 0.02625 = 65.6 a week (+75%); utilization 65.6 ÷ 250 hosts = 0.26: still healthy. At a $120 average booking and a 12% take rate, weekly revenue rises from 37.5 × $120 × 0.12 = $540 to 65.6 × $120 × 0.12 = $945.</p>\n<p><strong>The guardrail.</strong> If the team added 400 hosts (550 in all) and availability rose only to 75%, bookings would be about 70 and utilization 0.13 bookings per host per week: half the current level, and hosts would likely leave. Adding supply beyond the demand it can serve strands hosts. So add in steps, check utilization after each, and watch quality (ratings, cancellations).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Splitting the booking rate into availability and conversion found the binding side, and utilization set a limit on the fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-worked-example-2-enterprise-pipeline-math-and-net-revenue-re\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Enterprise: pipeline math and net revenue retention (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Pipeline (FW-C27-03).</strong> A sales-led product sells at an annual contract value (ACV) of $60,000, wins 25% of qualified opportunities, and has a nine-month sales cycle (invented). To close 10 deals, the team needs 10 ÷ 0.25 = 40 qualified opportunities, worth $600,000 in new annual contract value if all 10 close. Because the cycle is nine months, opportunities opened now close next year, so a shortfall in pipeline shows up in revenue nine months later: the PM watches the pipeline, win rate and cycle time, not only closed revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Net revenue retention.</strong> A cohort of 100 customers paying $1,000,000 a year in total. Over the year: customers who leave take away $80,000; downgrades reduce revenue by $30,000; expansion (more seats and products) adds $210,000. NRR = (1,000,000 − 80,000 − 30,000 + 210,000) ÷ 1,000,000 = 110%. The cohort grew 10% with no new customers. Logo churn (the share of customers lost) is a separate measure: here 8% of revenue came from leaving customers, and a count of logos could differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Land and expand.</strong> A team lands with 20 seats at $12 per seat per month: $2,880 a year. If usage spreads to 200 seats, the account is $28,800 a year, ten times larger, with little sales effort if adoption is organic. This is how a product-led entry (C20.5) produces high NRR; it requires that the product be useful to the first team without a long implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Buyers and users.</strong> The 20 users love the product; the economic buyer needs a security review, single sign-on and an audit log before approving 200 seats. If the roadmap ignores those, the deal stalls in the buying map (FW-C27-03) despite user love. The PM treats these requirements as product work with a deadline set by the sales cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Pipeline and cycle math showed why enterprise revenue lags, NRR showed growth without new logos, and the buying map showed that non-feature requirements decide deals.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-worked-example-3-fraud-screening-versus-false-declines-illus\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Fraud screening versus false declines (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C27-04) (invented).</strong> A payments product processes 1,000,000 card payments in a period, average $80, with a 0.4% fraud rate (4,000 fraudulent). Each missed fraud costs $95 (the amount plus a $15 chargeback fee). The product's margin is 3% ($2.40 per payment). A false decline (a good payment wrongly blocked) loses the margin and some future value: suppose $6 of lost future contribution, so $8.40 per false decline (the $6 is an assumption).</p>\n<p><strong>Two screening settings.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting</th>\n<th>Fraud caught</th>\n<th>Good payments wrongly declined</th>\n<th>Missed fraud</th>\n<th>Cost of missed fraud</th>\n<th>Cost of false declines</th>\n<th>Total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tight</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>1.5% (14,940)</td>\n<td>1,200</td>\n<td>$114,000</td>\n<td>$125,496</td>\n<td>$239,496</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loose</td>\n<td>55%</td>\n<td>0.5% (4,980)</td>\n<td>1,800</td>\n<td>$171,000</td>\n<td>$41,832</td>\n<td>$212,832</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>With no screening the loss would be 4,000 × $95 = $380,000. Both settings help; the loose setting costs $26,664 less under these assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>What decides it.</strong> The cost of a false decline. The tight setting declines 9,960 more good payments than the loose one and catches 600 more frauds (worth $57,000). The break-even cost per false decline is $57,000 ÷ 9,960 = $5.72: if a false decline costs more than that in total (margin plus lost future value), the loose setting is cheaper; if less, the tight one is. With only the $2.40 margin counted, the tight setting would win ($149,856 against $182,952). So an estimate of the lifetime effect of a false decline settles the policy, and it should be measured (a holdout that approves a sample of borderline payments gives the true fraud rate among them).</p>\n<p><strong>Better design.</strong> A single threshold wastes the middle: approve clear cases, block clear fraud, and add friction (a verification step) for the borderline band, which converts some false declines into approvals and some fraud into catches (C25.4 layers).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A cost comparison with a break-even showed that the fraud policy turns on a number (the cost of a false decline) that many teams never measure.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-worked-example-4-prevalence-and-thresholds-in-trust-and-safe\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Prevalence and thresholds in trust and safety (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C27-05) (invented).</strong> A video surface has 1,000,000 views a day. Sampling and review estimate that 0.15% of views are of content that violates a policy: 1,500 violating views a day (prevalence 0.15%).</p>\n<p><strong>Setting A.</strong> The detection system removes violating content with 70% recall and 90% precision. Violations removed: 0.70 × 1,500 = 1,050. Wrongly removed (false positives): 1,050 × (10 ÷ 90) = about 117. Remaining violating views: 450, so prevalence falls to 0.045%, a 70% reduction. Human review: about 1,167 flagged items a day; at 100 items per reviewer-day, about 12 reviewers.</p>\n<p><strong>Setting B.</strong> A stricter threshold: 85% recall and 80% precision. Violations removed: 0.85 × 1,500 = 1,275; wrongly removed: 1,275 × (20 ÷ 80) = about 319; remaining 225, so prevalence 0.0225%. Review load rises to about 1,594 flagged a day (about 16 reviewers).</p>\n<p><strong>The tradeoff.</strong> Moving from A to B halves the remaining violating views (450 to 225) and nearly triples wrongful removals (117 to 319), with 4 more reviewers. Whether that is right depends on the harm of each error. For a severe harm (child safety), B or stricter is justified, with appeals to correct errors; for a borderline category (a mild policy), A may be better. The threshold is a policy decision (C23.3), set per harm.</p>\n<p><strong>What to measure alongside.</strong> Prevalence by sampling (not removals, which rise when detection improves); the appeal and overturn rate (false removals); proactive detection rate; time to action; and coverage across languages and groups, since detection often works worse in some languages.</p>\n<p><strong>Adversaries.</strong> Abusers adapt (new wording, images), so recall measured today falls over time; refresh sampling monthly and red-team the detector.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Prevalence gave an honest measure of harm, and the two settings made the tradeoff between harm remaining and false removals explicit and per harm.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-worked-example-5-how-an-ad-auction-picks-and-prices-an-ad-il\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — How an ad auction picks and prices an ad (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C27-06).</strong> Three advertisers compete for one ad slot (invented). Each has a bid per click and a predicted click rate: A bids $2.00 with a predicted 1.0%; B bids $3.00 with 0.5%; C bids $1.50 with 1.2%.</p>\n<p><strong>Scores.</strong> Bid × predicted click rate (expected revenue per impression): A 0.0200; B 0.0150; C 0.0180. Ranking: A first, C second, B third. B has the highest bid and loses because users are unlikely to click its ad.</p>\n<p><strong>The price.</strong> The winner pays the least that would keep its position: A needs a score above C's 0.0180, so its price per click is 0.0180 ÷ 0.010 = $1.80 (not its $2.00 bid). Expected revenue per thousand impressions: at its price, 1,000 × 0.010 × $1.80 = $18.00 (at its bid it would be $20.00).</p>\n<p><strong>Why weight by predicted response.</strong> Ranking by bid alone would show B's ad: expected revenue per thousand impressions 1,000 × 0.005 × $3.00 = $15.00 (at its bid), lower than A's $18.00 at price, and the user would see a less relevant ad. Weighting by predicted response raises revenue, protects the user experience and rewards advertisers with relevant ads.</p>\n<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Predicted rates must be calibrated (C23): if the model overestimates an ad's rate, the platform loses revenue and shows poor ads. Watch ad load, retention and complaints, because more ads raise short-term revenue and can reduce return visits (C20.6). Privacy changes that reduce targeting signal lower predicted response and advertiser results.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A three-bidder example explained that the auction ranks by expected value, prices at the next competitor's level and serves users and advertisers together.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Authorization rates on your payments product fell by 1.2 points last week. How do you diagnose it?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd look at the data to find what changed, maybe a bug, and fix it. I'd also check with the banks.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no structure, no segmentation, no thought of fraud settings or partner rules, no estimate of impact.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd size it: 1.2 points on, say, 500,000 weekly attempts of $50 at a 2.5% take rate is about $7,500 a week. Then segment: by issuer and card network, country, payment method, merchant, device, amount and time. Hypotheses: a release or configuration change on our side, a change in our fraud screening that declines more, an issuer or network issue, a change in merchant mix, or a partner outage. The cheapest check is the release and configuration log against the date, then whether the drop is concentrated in one issuer or one segment.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd separate declines by who made them: our own fraud rules, the issuer, or a technical failure, since the fixes differ. If the drop is in our fraud declines, I'd check the threshold or a new rule and compare the cost of the lost approvals with the fraud avoided: on a million attempts, tight screening might decline 1.5% of good payments against 0.5% for a looser setting, and the break-even depends on what a false decline costs us. If one issuer is declining more, I'd contact them with transaction examples, and add retries or alternative routing for those payments. I'd check for a mix shift: a new merchant with riskier traffic can lower the aggregate rate with nothing broken. I'd set a guardrail so that a fix does not raise fraud or chargebacks, and I'd keep a holdout to measure the true fraud rate among declined borderline payments. Finally, I'd put an alert on authorization rate by issuer, so that the next drop is seen in hours.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c27-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sizes the impact and segments the data</td>\n<td>Separates who declined (us, issuer, technical)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lists hypotheses with a cheapest check</td>\n<td>Compares fraud and false-decline costs; mix shift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mentions release logs and partners</td>\n<td>Guardrails, a holdout, alerts by segment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c27-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Products and interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google's businesses include advertising, a cloud platform sold to enterprises, devices and consumer services <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet Form 10-K for 2025]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on ads, platforms and trust at scale <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: be ready with the ad-auction basics, the platform adoption view and the safety tradeoffs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n";
